{"benchmark": "livebench_selected", "task_id": "paraphrase:f3472a74bc480a915c2a3875c505fd5b3ebe475d25cefdae9447002ed88b0106", "sample_idx": 2, "model_name": "abacusai/dracarys-llama-3.1-70b-instruct", "content": "Oregon's pioneering effort to decriminalize drugs will come to an end on Sunday, as a new law, HB4002, will once again make it a crime to possess small amounts of hard drugs. Those caught with illicit substances will have the option to either face charges or undergo treatment, which includes completing a behavioral health program and participating in a deflection program to avoid fines.\n\nPersonal-use possession will be considered a misdemeanor, punishable by up to six months in jail. The new law aims to make it easier for police to address drug use in public and introduces harsher penalties for selling drugs near public spaces.\n\nThe law encourages, but does not require, counties to create treatment alternatives to divert people from the criminal justice system and towards addiction and mental health services. Supporters argue that this allows counties to develop programs based on their resources, while opponents claim that it may lead to a confusing and inequitable patchwork of policy.\n\nThe criteria for deflection are narrow, and if a person meets the requirements, officers will contact the county's deflection dispatch line to provide basic information and connect them to a mobile behavioral health outreach team. If the team cannot make contact within 30 minutes, the person will be taken to jail.\n\n***\n***", "logprobs": [{"token": "Oregon", "logprob": -0.02715725265443325}, {"token": "'s", "logprob": -0.11135509610176086}, {"token": " pioneering", "logprob": -0.4637451171875}, {"token": " effort", "logprob": -1.771671175956726}, {"token": " to", "logprob": -0.0009558595484122634}, {"token": " dec", "logprob": -8.344653679159819e-07}, {"token": "riminal", "logprob": -3.576279254957626e-07}, {"token": "ize", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " drugs", "logpr